::rlc::ir::typecheck
{
	ENUM Primitive {
		u1, u2, u3, u4, u5, u6, u7, u8,
		s1, s2, s3, s4, s5, s6, s7, s8
	}

	ENUM OverflowMode {
		debug,
		ok,
		allow,
		truncate,
		check
	}

	ENUM DivMode { // TODO: redo.
		safe,
		check,
		allow_0
	}

	// Operation that needs to be performed by the static executor or generated by the code generator. This is the output the typechecker generates. Ideally, this already contains all the info needed to branchlessly execute it (such as field offsets or something). Actually, we do not want this as data, but we want to directly call these on the target, so the target can produce the perfect representation. But then again, we ourselves already want to be branchless and parallel. So the code that generates this would already need to be table driven and branchless and those tables etc. would have to be supplied by the target. Which is a good thing long-term, as it reduces work and reduces friction. But we can only really get to that once we built a few backends.
	/// Retarded (???) idea: try compiling on the GPU, if we manage to make it massively interleaved & branchless anyway. Although then we might have to generate code that the GPU can run, or we run the generated static code on the CPU and pass results back.
	/// Observation: fully branchless and interleaved state machines turn into some kind of circuit simulator or interpreter circuit. So we seem to actually be trying to design & emulate very simple and efficient interpreter circuits and feed them bytecode programs, instead of executing native logic, because that would branch, and can't be SIMD-ised well. But for these very simple circuits, we can interleave them to hide latency, and then also increase throughput via actual SIMD.
	/// TODO: how can this observation be leveraged for our own ISA? A single-stepping virtualisation or something could maybe help, where you execute one instruction directly from immediates, or in some kind of pre-defined memory structure. That would be trying to make polymorphic heterogeneous execution possible. The other way would be trying to make circuits somehow easier to execute or some other kind of acceleration structure for circuits. A fundamental part of these circuits is that they consume an input stream, have no or minimal state, and produce an output stream, and have almost trivial logic, even if at the cost of more input commands.
		/// Ideal requirements for these circuits: We iterate linearly over interleaved input streams, and circuit states are also packed so that we can SIMD-ise them fully, and also interleave multiple SIMD executions if possible. We want a very low amortised cost per operation, all operations need to be branchless, and the branchlessness should have minimal overhead of additional operations. We want to have interleaved input and output streams if possible, although it is usually not possible. There is a cost when switching from SIMD to scalar execution, which makes the SIMD execution pointless on x86 in many cases. Variable-speed outputs are bad for that. If we can have uniform-speed outputs, then that changes things. Interleaving of outputs can become a problem if we do not want to access the outputs in the same configuration as they were generated. Which is the case for templates etc. probably. Although we do want full type checking to the extent possible on all code anyway. Just template instantiation might then turn out a bit slower, but we could still benefit from faster initial type checking for everything else. If we had good gather instructions, that would not be an issue.
	/// TODO: figure out what kinds of circuit design work well for this mode of execution.
	UNION Operation
	{
		add: {Primitive, OverflowMode};
		sub: {Primitive, OverflowMode};
		mul: {Primitive, OverflowMode};
		div: {Primitive, DivMode};

		pass_arg_temp_addr: {U1, TempID};
		pass_arg_temp_value: {U1, TempID};
		pass_arg_imm: {U1, U8};

		call: Function;
		dereference: Type;

		unresolved: TAGGED UnresolvedOperation;

		fixedNumberLoopStart: U8;
		variableNumberLoopStart: TempID;
		sliceLoopStart: TempID;
		loopCond: TempID;
		loopStep;
		loopStart;
		loopEnd;
		continue: U1;
		break: U1;
		breakable: :jumpTarget;
	}

	UNION TypeCheckerOperation {
		LookupPointer;
		LookupFunction;
		LookupTable;
		Write;
	}

	ENUM ResolveNameConstraint {
		Scope,
		Type,
		Value
	}

	(// Scopes:
		- All files share one global scope for now. Later on, we need to be careful about including multiple files that define entities with the same name, as they are individually valid, but no longer valid when included together. We want to switch to the new file scoping as soon as possible, because it allows us to compile a lot of code in parallel.
		- Problem: we need to efficiently look up whether an identifier is in a scope. We could do this by using a bloom and a hash or something as a heuristic. And then we need an efficient representation for the actual identifiers and their associated values. Ideally, we either have a generic scope structure, or we pass unresolved symbols to the parent scope. As soon as we have merged scopes based on includes, this will become quite tricky, as we want to have a scope that has inheriting scopes but also detects loops. And then we have public/private versions of scopes and all that. Maybe it would really be the easiest if we had one public and one private version, and then it just refers to some per-file table or something. That would make at least that simple. And then we need to think of include cycles that have some private links. Technically, only public include cycles count, as private includes are not exported again. We should therefore also keep per-file state instead of per-cycle state, probably, or both. So we first do local variable name resolution in the parser, which already eliminates a lot. Then we identify include clusters/cycles of files, and isolate them, and merge them, and process them in parallel. TODO: How exactly do private includes affect clusters/cycles?

			- All public declarations are in global scope, but need to be imported to be accessible. There must not be any conflicting public declarations anywhere in the program, even if they are not in the same include.
			- All private declarations are in file scope, and are not exported. Private declarations can conflict with private declarations in other files, but cannot conflict with public declarations.
				- This means we first have to process all public declarations in all files, and then have to process all private declarations afterwards to see whether they conflict with any public declaration. Or maybe a private declaration is allowed to conflict with a public declaration if it is not included from the current file. That would allow us to shadow public interfaces or something, maybe? But would be a bit useless maybe.
				- For now, we only implement public imports and public declarations, and have one global scope. Later on, we add private declarations. If we then need to, we may add private imports, but maybe they aren't even useful. Or maybe they are?
			- Dependencies are imported as a reserved namespace that we cannot reuse or extend in our program.

			- public cycles create a cluster where all files are identical.
			- private cycles only create a dependency but files have different scopes.
	/)

	(/ Compiler workflow:
		1. Parsing:
			1. Detect all included files, load those.
			2. Tokenise all files.
			3. Parse structure:
				- Populate global scope.
				- Resolve local variable names.
		2. Resolve all non-local identifiers.
		3. Typecheck and instantiate AST-code:
			- Compile time evaluation of constant expressions
			- Output TIR (templated IR, partially resolved)
				- TIR should also generate the code that is required to instantiate the code, so that it is just one function pointer call or something. If it does not need instantiation (no templates), then it just generates the code directly, unless inlined. Inlined code becomes a resolved AST-code that is simply inserted into the calling function.
			- Instantiate TIR to IR
		4. Output code /)

	Resolver {
		// Batchable per file, or even across files, if we have a generic scope structure that is layered. Not sure whether interleaved binary search would be worth it, but could be worth it. Or just one linear search over the scope. But actually, we would not profit much from that, because it just doesn't scale well for many values. We would have N comparisons per scope entry, for N symbols to resolve. For binary search, we interleave everything and we are done in logN steps and can hide the latency and if we make it branchless, we also do not have branch misses really.
		ResolveRootName: {Identifier, ResolveNameConstraint};
		// Batchable per function / class, or per file, too, if we have a recursive scope structure. But we cannot do interleaved binary search very well on that. It could be better to just do it once per scope, with shared search space, etc. Or maybe we simply propagate the task downwards to the common scope if it didn't work.
		ResolveRelativeName: {Identifier, ResolveNameConstraint};
		// only works if interleaved with the others.
		ResolveSubNameOfLastResolved: {Identifier, ResolveNameConstraint}; // To keep the union small, we require the resolver to keep track of the last resolved item.

		(/EMBED/) InstantiatorAction: IrInstantiator; // probably only a subset, as this comes straight from the 
	}

	// Wir brauchen gather-scatter, deshalb kein SIMD.
	[#kN]TypeCheckerCircuits {
		Output: TAGGED Operation[#][kN]; // Könnte man vielleicht auch als ein interleaved array machen, damit weniger cache lines aktiv sind.
	}

	// Do we even have operands here? We could just pack it straight into the operation.


	(/ Structure:
		- Code gets parsed into:
			- global scope tree
			- bytecode for the IR0
		- IR0 executes the code (resolving names and stuff?)
			- Generates code that the typechecker needs to run.
			- Generates code that the static executor needs to run? The typechecker needs to know static expressions like array sizes etc., probably. But static execution also depends on the type checker. So either the typechecker needs to typecheck and also execute stuff (including potentially stuff like compile time functions), OR we make it so that the type checker simply iterates multiple times, and each time generates tasks for the executor to execute.
				- Then the type checker needs to be less smart and we can probably interleave multiple executions better. Then we can use the code generator to generate batches of code for the static executor to execute, and then we can efficiently execute code at compile time while also making use of the interleaved nature of the code generator. We do this until we have fully analysed a function, and then we can generate the runtime code for it. This is also potentially a separate code generator from the static executor, since it might target a different platform.
	/)
}